        </property>
        <layout class="QVBoxLayout" name="quality_layout">
         <property name="spacing">
          <number>1</number>
         </property>
         <property name="leftMargin">
          <number>0</number>
         </property>
         <property name="topMargin">
          <number>0</number>
         </property>
         <property name="rightMargin">
          <number>0</number>
         </property>
         <property name="bottomMargin">
          <number>0</number>
         </property>
         <item>
          <widget class="ComboBox" name="cmb_resolution">
           <property name="minimumSize">
            <size>
             <width>180</width>
             <height>19</height>
            </size>
           </property>
           <property name="maximumSize">
            <size>
             <width>999</width>
             <height>19</height>
            </size>
           </property>
           <property name="toolTip">
            <string>Lightmap resolution (square, px): each object's map, or the shared atlas. Drives the gutter / seam-margin width too.

The filter button beside the field denoises every map (Maya: at the size it ships at; Blender: with OpenImageDenoise).</string>
           </property>
           <property name="frame">
            <bool>false</bool>
           </property>
          </widget>
         </item>
         <item>
          <widget class="QSpinBox" name="spn_samples">
           <property name="minimumSize">
            <size>
             <width>0</width>
             <height>19</height>
            </size>
           </property>
           <property name="maximumSize">
            <size>
             <width>16777215</width>
             <height>19</height>
            </size>
           </property>
           <property name="toolTip">
            <string>Samples per texel. Higher = cleaner, slower.
Maya: Arnold camera (AA) samples, squared. On the CPU each one traces GI Samples diffuse rays. Arnold's GPU ignores GI samples, so a GPU bake gives every texel these samples and, with Adaptive Sampling on, adds more — up to Samples × GI Samples — only where the noise needs them (the shadows).
Blender: Cycles samples (paths). One count covers direct and bounced light alike — Cycles has no separate GI samples.

The button beside the field is Adaptive Sampling: the budget goes where the noise is. It greys out where the processor spends samples the other way (an Arnold CPU bake).</string>
           </property>
           <property name="prefix">
            <string>Samples:	</string>
           </property>
           <property name="minimum">
            <number>1</number>
           </property>
           <property name="maximum">
            <number>256</number>
           </property>
           <property name="value">
            <number>4</number>
           </property>
          </widget>
         </item>
         <item>
          <widget class="QSpinBox" name="spn_gi_samples">
           <property name="minimumSize">
            <size>
             <width>0</width>
             <height>19</height>
            </size>
           </property>
           <property name="maximumSize">
            <size>
             <width>16777215</width>
             <height>19</height>
            </size>
           </property>
           <property name="toolTip">
            <string>Arnold's GI (indirect diffuse) samples, squared (Maya only: Cycles traces every path from Samples). On the CPU: the bounce-light rays each camera sample traces. On the GPU, which ignores them, they set the budget instead: Samples × GI Samples is Adaptive Sampling's ceiling, or what every texel gets with it off. Higher = smoother bounce light and shadows, slower.</string>
           </property>
           <property name="prefix">
            <string>GI Samples:	</string>
           </property>
           <property name="minimum">
            <number>1</number>
           </property>
           <property name="maximum">
            <number>32</number>
           </property>
           <property name="value">
            <number>2</number>
           </property>
          </widget>
         </item>
         <item>
          <widget class="QSpinBox" name="spn_bounces">
           <property name="minimumSize">
            <size>
             <width>0</width>
             <height>19</height>
            </size>
           </property>
           <property name="maximumSize">
            <size>
             <width>16777215</width>
             <height>19</height>
            </size>
           </property>
           <property name="toolTip">
            <string>Diffuse bounces (Maya: Arnold's GI Diffuse Depth; Blender: Cycles' Light Paths › Max Bounces › Diffuse): how many times light bounces between surfaces before the bake stops following it. 0 = direct light only; 2–4 lights a room's corners and ceiling. The biggest quality lever for interiors — each bounce adds render time.</string>
           </property>
           <property name="prefix">
            <string>Bounces:	</string>
           </property>
           <property name="minimum">
            <number>0</number>
           </property>
           <property name="maximum">
            <number>16</number>
           </property>
           <property name="value">
            <number>4</number>
           </property>
          </widget>
         </item>
        </layout>
       </widget>
      </item>
      <item>
